# MASTER DOCKER — 16 Essential Concepts

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| No. | Docker Concept            | Purpose                                                                                                                |
| --- | ------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 01  | Containerization          | Packages an application and its dependencies into isolated containers                                                  |
| 02  | Docker Architecture       | Explains how the Docker client, daemon, APIs, and registries work together                                             |
| 03  | Docker Engine             | Builds and runs containers and manages Docker resources                                                                |
| 04  | Docker Images             | Read-only templates used to create containers                                                                          |
| 05  | Docker Containers         | Running or stopped instances of Docker images                                                                          |
| 06  | Dockerfile                | Defines instructions to build a Docker image                                                                           |
| 07  | Docker Image Layers       | Stores image changes in reusable layers to improve build efficiency                                                    |
| 08  | Docker Volumes            | Persists data independently of a container's lifecycle                                                                 |
| 09  | Bind Mounts               | Maps a specific host file or directory into a container                                                                |
| 10  | Docker Networking         | Enables communication between containers and external systems                                                          |
| 11  | Docker Bridge Network     | Connects containers through a virtual network, commonly on a single host                                               |
| 12  | Docker Host Network       | Allows a container to use the host's network namespace                                                                 |
| 13  | Docker Compose            | Defines and manages multi-container applications using a YAML file                                                     |
| 14  | Docker Registry           | Stores and distributes Docker images                                                                                   |
| 15  | Docker Hub and Amazon ECR | Docker Hub is a public/private image registry; Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) is AWS's managed image registry |
| 16  | Multi-Stage Builds        | Uses multiple build stages to create smaller, more efficient production images                                         |
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Recommended learning order ##

Phase 1 — Fundamentals : Containerization, architecture, Docker Engine, images, containers, Dockerfile, image layers.

Phase 2 — Data and networking : Volumes, bind mounts, networking, bridge network, host network.

Phase 3 — Production workflows : Docker Compose, registries, Docker Hub, Amazon ECR, multi-stage builds.


## Essential Docker commands to practice ##

docker --version
docker pull nginx
docker images
docker build -t my-app .
docker run -d -p 8080:80 --name web nginx
docker ps
docker ps -a
docker logs web
docker exec -it web sh
docker stop web
docker start web
docker rm web
docker rmi nginx
docker volume ls
docker network ls
docker compose up -d
docker compose down


Goal: Build a Docker image for a Node.js application, run it locally, connect it to a database using Docker Compose,
      persist database data with a volume, and publish the image to Docker Hub or Amazon ECR. 
      This is a strong practical foundation for DevOps and cloud deployment.
